# Conjunctive queries an optimiser has to compare, and the three questions it
# is really asking:
#
#   1. Is this self-join redundant?      (Q_verbose vs Q_terse)
#   2. Can I answer this from that view? (Q_us_orders vs Q_any_region)
#   3. Is a one-hop query the same as a two-hop one? (Q_edge vs Q_path2)
#
# Each is an instance of conjunctive-query containment, NP-complete, and each
# answer here carries a certificate: a homomorphism when the containment
# holds, and a concrete counterexample database -- the frozen body of the
# left query -- when it does not.
#
# Vocabulary: `Placed(order, customer)`, `Ships(order, warehouse)`,
# `Located(customer, region)`, `Edge(from, to)`. A lowercase-initial argument
# is a variable; `US` is a constant, and constants are not mapped by a
# homomorphism, which is exactly why question 2 is asymmetric.

# 1. The second `Placed` atom binds a fresh variable and constrains nothing.
#    The two queries are EQUIVALENT: containment holds in both directions, so
#    dropping the atom is sound. This is Chandra-Merlin query minimisation.
query Q_verbose(cust) :- Placed(o, cust), Placed(o, cust2), Ships(o, wh)
query Q_terse(cust)   :- Placed(o, cust), Ships(o, wh)

expect subset : Q_verbose Q_terse
expect subset : Q_terse Q_verbose

# 2. A materialised view over every region subsumes the US-only query, so the
#    US query may be answered from the view. The reverse fails, and the
#    counterexample database shows why in two facts.
query Q_us_orders(o)  :- Placed(o, cust), Located(cust, US)
query Q_any_region(o) :- Placed(o, cust), Located(cust, region)

expect subset    : Q_us_orders Q_any_region
expect notsubset : Q_any_region Q_us_orders

# 3. Two hops are not one hop, and one hop is not two. Neither containment
#    holds, and each direction gets its own two-fact and one-fact database.
query Q_path2(x, z) :- Edge(x, y), Edge(y, z)
query Q_edge(x, z)  :- Edge(x, z)

expect notsubset : Q_path2 Q_edge
expect notsubset : Q_edge Q_path2
